^2: y = 4-2x\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $P(1, 2)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n\n- The point $P(1, 2)$ belongs to $A$\n\n- The point $Q(0,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" 13194139534768 17592186045417 false] [17592186124199 1071 "The set $A$ and its boundary $\\partial A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=\\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2: y > 4-2x\\big\\}, \\qquad \\partial A = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2: y = 4-2x\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The point $P(1, 2)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n\nII. The point $P(1, 2)$ belongs to $A$\n\nIII. The point $Q(0,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" 13194139534768 17592186045417 true] [17592186124200 1071 "The set $A$ and its boundary $\\partial A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=\\bigg\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2:  \\dfrac{x^2}{4} + \\dfrac{y^2}{8} \\geq 1 \\bigg\\}, \\qquad \\partial A = \\bigg\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2:  \\dfrac{x^2}{4} + \\dfrac{y^2}{8} =1 \\bigg\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $Q\\left(-\\sqrt 2,2\\right)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n\n- The point $P(1, 1)$ belongs to $A$\n\n- The point $Q\\left(-\\sqrt 2,2\\right)$ is an interior point of $A$" 13194139534768 17592186045417 false] [17592186124200 1071 "The set $A$ and its boundary $\\partial A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=\\bigg\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2:  \\dfrac{x^2}{4} + \\dfrac{y^2}{8} \\geq 1 \\bigg\\}, \\qquad \\partial A = \\bigg\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2:  \\dfrac{x^2}{4} + \\dfrac{y^2}{8} =1 \\bigg\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The point $Q\\left(-\\sqrt 2,2\\right)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n\nII. The point $P(1, 1)$ belongs to $A$\n\nIII. The point $Q\\left(-\\sqrt 2,2\\right)$ is an interior point of $A$" 13194139534768 17592186045417 true] [17592186124635 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435511 13194139534768 17592186045417 true] [17592186124723 1050 480.0 13194139534768 17592186045417 true] [17592186124809 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534768 17592186045417 true] [17592186124809 1071 "![](images/51/51ccbc1cf370686432f144f9bf3f8f389b5ee1beb652b704266708fc1e9d8e6d.png)\n\nConsider the set $M = {0}∪(2, ∞)$ and the points $A$, $B$, and $C$, shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $A$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n2. $B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n3. $C$ is an interior point of the set $M$" 13194139534768 17592186045417 false] [17592186124809 1071 "![](images/51/51ccbc1cf370686432f144f9bf3f8f389b5ee1beb652b704266708fc1e9d8e6d.png)\n\nConsider the set $M=\\{0\\}\\cup (2,\\infty )$ and the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n\nII. $B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n\nIII. $C$ is an interior point of the set $M$" 13194139534768 17592186045417 true] [17592186124809 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}\\in R$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is *smaller than* $\\delta :$ \n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nA neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is given by the open interval $({x}_{0}-\\delta ,{x}_{0}+\\delta ),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f889352f25e7936d0e5ea76585a979efbb301da61f7e99a937e979af93ee0664.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The point $A$ is *not* an interior point of $M$ since any neighborhood of $A$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\n![](images/ba/baece8024a1c90a9d83efc2669ecbdda20867f0cebc31670b80deadd36526cf2.png)\n\n- Statement II is false. The point $B$ is *not* a boundary point since there exists any neighborhood of $B$ that is fully contained *outside* the set $M.$\n\n![](images/19/1943ab4d5fa1cc8358352e325e1dd44d48c45922dabf09813a8568266e466cf7.png)\n\n- Statement III is true. The point $C$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n![](images/01/01c22233e2876de03185de67738b46116f70e9084d57ea6c0884fc483e920c81.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'III only.'" 13194139534768 17592186045417 true] [17592186125863 1064 "A **neighborhood** of a point $x_0\\in \\mathbb R$ is the set of points that are \"close to\" $x_0.$\n\nMore precisely, let's define a number $\\delta >0$, where $\\delta$ is usually very small. A **neighborhood** of a point $x_0$ is the set of all points whose distance from $x_0$ is smaller than $\\delta.$\n\nIn one-dimensional space, a neighborhood of a point $x_0\\in\\mathbb R$ is an open interval $\\left(x_0-\\delta, x_0+\\delta\\right),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/2e/2e28aded47a5f57c88b15a05ea032ec05527c0a47a6e5a72f029c5b5d6d9cc61.svg)\n\n![](images/04/04016c37be4c907eabc144db16eb757dff773907262ccd04941cf27ddd2e49d6.png)\n\n![](images/e9/e9455f39338547fa24cf28db5fbf3a925c8dedc6b6d9fe2d1411cc0139f9bdf5.svg)\n\nFor example, if we set $\\delta = 0.1,$ then the corresponding neighborhood of the point $x_0 = 2$ is as follows:\n\n![](images/08/08364d92317893fc21a0af75ccf3c824314e38edbb8a75e23b2206eebf191161.svg)\n\n![](images/03/0329bef56b8b903eb13510b7344fd7ca488d34c8d0c85c7648cebc8ecf7cfa40.png)\n\n![](images/f8/f8bddff4ee56ec85ef47a3f4404de1443a7d2c93491acb1933d97168d5092b7f.svg)\n\nNote that $x=1.9$ and $x=2.1$ are *not* included in this neighborhood since their distance from the point $x_0$ is *not* smaller than $\\delta.$\n\nWe can represent a neighborhood of $x_0$ using set notation as follows:\n\n$$\\{x : | x- x_0| < \\delta \\}$$\n\nWe often refer to sets of this type as *open* neighborhoods since they contain all points whose distance from $x_0$ is *smaller than* (but not equal) to $\\delta.$" 13194139534768 17592186045417 false] [17592186125863 1064 "A **neighborhood** of a point $x_0\\in \\mathbb R$ is the set of points that are \"close to\" $x_0.$ \n\nMore precisely, let's define a number $\\delta >0$, where $\\delta$ is usually very small.  A **neighborhood** of a point $x_0$ is the set of all points whose distance from $x_0$ is smaller than $\\delta.$ \n\nIn one-dimensional space, a neighborhood of a point $x_0\\in\\mathbb R$ is an open interval $\\left(x_0-\\delta, x_0+\\delta\\right),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/04/04016c37be4c907eabc144db16eb757dff773907262ccd04941cf27ddd2e49d6.png)\n\nFor example, if we set $\\delta = 0.1,$ then the corresponding neighborhood of the point $x_0 = 2$ is as follows:\n\n![](images/03/0329bef56b8b903eb13510b7344fd7ca488d34c8d0c85c7648cebc8ecf7cfa40.png)\n\nNote that $x=1.9$ and $x=2.1$ are *not* included in this neighborhood since their distance from the point $x_0$ is *not* smaller than $\\delta.$ \n\nWe can represent a neighborhood of $x_0$ using set notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x : | x- x_0| < \\delta \\}\n$$\n\nWe often refer to sets of this type as *open* neighborhoods since they contain all points whose distance from $x_0$ is *smaller than* (but not equal) to $\\delta.$" 13194139534768 17592186045417 true] [17592186125864 1064 "In two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of a point $\\mathbf x_0\\in \\mathbb R^2$ is a disc of radius $\\delta$ centered at $\\mathbf x_0$ that does not include the bounding circle, as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nThe definitions of the interior and boundary points are the same as for the one-dimensional case. The only difference is that now a neighborhood of a point is a disc.\n\nFor example, consider the set $S$ and points $A,$ $B, C,$ and $D,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/a7/a7e037fd998c235ff7b721e7187d44814700ac5f41a256920f34f248c798c520.png)\n\nLet's classify each of these as interior or boundary points (or neither):\n\n- $A$ is an interior point since *there exists* a neighborhood of $A$ that is fully contained inside the set.\n\n- $B$ is neither an interior nor a boundary point because *there exists* a neighborhood of $B$ that does not contain points from the set.\n\n- $C$ is a boundary point since *every* neighborhood of $C$ contains points that lie in the set and points that do not lie in the set.\n\n- $D$ is a boundary point since *every* neighborhood of $D$ contains points that lie in the set and points that do not lie in the set.\n\nNotice that although $C$ and $D$ are both boundary points, $C\\notin S$ and yet $D\\in S.$" 13194139534768 17592186045417 true] [17592186125864 1064 "In two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of a point $\\mathbf x_0\\in \\mathbb R^2$ is a disc of radius $\\delta$ centered at $\\mathbf x_0$ that does not include the bounding circle, as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nThe definitions of the interior and boundary points are the same as for the one-dimensional case. The only difference is that now a neighborhood of a point is a disc.\n\nFor example, consider the set $S$ and points $A,$ $B, C,$ and $D,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/a7/a7e037fd998c235ff7b721e7187d44814700ac5f41a256920f34f248c798c520.png)\n\nLet's classify each of these as interior or boundary points (or neither):\n\n- $A$ is an interior point since *there exists* a neighborhood of $A$ that is fully contained inside the set.\n- $B$ is neither an interior nor a boundary point because *there exists* a neighborhood of $B$ that does not contain points from the set.\n- $C$ is a boundary point since *every* neighborhood of $C$ contains points that lie in the set and points that do not lie in the set.\n- $D$ is a boundary point since *every* neighborhood of $D$ contains points that lie in the set and points that do not lie in the set.\n\nNotice that although $C$ and $D$ are both boundary points, $C\\notin S$ and yet $D\\in S.$" 13194139534768 17592186045417 false] [17592186125870 1064 "Now, suppose that $S$ is a set of real numbers. A point $x_0$ is called an **interior point** of $S$ if *some* neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0$ is *fully contained* inside $S.$\n\nFor example, consider the set $S=[0,1)$ and the points $A$ and $B,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/22/22a3429a80227c296fb911ce0caefd282e4066c70da10cf9fe9740a5523a0da7.svg)\n\n![](images/a1/a12c60b6f60ca7c35162fb1ddf0d0d507fe80e73e621c451c21f0b882034ae21.png)\n\n![](images/2e/2e28aded47a5f57c88b15a05ea032ec05527c0a47a6e5a72f029c5b5d6d9cc61.svg)\n\nThe point $A$ is an interior point of $S,$ but $B$ is not:\n\n- The point $A$ is an interior point of $S$ since there exists a neighborhood of $A$ that is *fully contained* inside $S.$\n- The point $B$ is *not* an interior point of $S$ since there is no neighborhood of $B$ that is *fully contained* inside $S.$ In other words, any neighborhood of $B$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nNote that neither $x=0$ nor $x=1$ are interior points. To see why, let's draw a neighborhood of each point:\n\n![](images/f3/f320e2e063366c71293fb0d5433ccd75ef5794dce9c69f9780c52610823b13a8.svg)\n\n![](images/a9/a923732241443a0fd6a5f53dab7de2d346f5704a0b034c276e64dc3df2746f4f.png)\n\nFrom here, we can deduce the following:\n\n- The point $x=0$ is *not* an interior point of $S$ since there is no neighborhood of $x=0$ that is *fully* contained inside $S.$\n- The point $x=1$ is also *not* an interior point of $S$ since there is no neighborhood of $x=1$ that is *fully* contained inside $S.$\n\nThe set containing all interior points of a set $S$ is called the **interior** of $S,$ and is denoted $\\textrm{int}\\,S.$\n\nFor the set $[0,1),$ its interior is given by\n\n$$\\textrm{int}\\,S = (0,1).$$" 13194139534768 17592186045417 false] [17592186125870 1064 "Now, suppose that $S$ is a set of real numbers. A point $x_0$ is called an **interior point** of $S$ if *some* neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0$ is *fully contained* inside $S.$ \n\nFor example, consider the set $S=[0,1)$ and the points $A$ and $B,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/a1/a12c60b6f60ca7c35162fb1ddf0d0d507fe80e73e621c451c21f0b882034ae21.png)\n\nThe point $A$ is an interior point of $S,$ but $B$ is not:\n\n- The point $A$ is an interior point of $S$ since there exists a neighborhood of $A$ that is *fully contained* inside $S.$\n\n- The point $B$ is *not* an interior point of $S$ since there is no neighborhood of $B$ that is *fully contained* inside $S.$ In other words, any neighborhood of $B$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nNote that neither $x=0$ nor $x=1$ are interior points. To see why, let's draw a neighborhood of each point:\n\n![](images/a9/a923732241443a0fd6a5f53dab7de2d346f5704a0b034c276e64dc3df2746f4f.png)\n\n \n\nFrom here, we can deduce the following:\n\n- The point $x=0$ is *not* an interior point of $S$ since there is no neighborhood of $x=0$ that is *fully* contained inside $S.$\n\n- The point $x=1$ is also *not* an interior point of $S$ since there is no neighborhood of $x=1$ that is *fully* contained inside $S.$\n\nThe set containing all interior points of a set $S$ is called the **interior** of $S,$ and is denoted $\\textrm{int}\\,S.$ \n\nFor the set $[0,1),$ its interior is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{int}\\,S = (0,1).\n$$" 13194139534768 17592186045417 true] [17592186125871 1064 "A point $x_0$ is a **boundary point** of $S$ if *every* neighborhood of $x_0$ contains points that *are* in $S$ *and* points that are *not* in $S.$\n\nLet's consider the set $S=[0,1),$ the points $x=0, x=1,$ and neighborhoods of these points.\n\n![](images/81/81d196b07cb70c7440bbf32cd23c2929b0e10f883f5bfdc90b087830dab5fdee.svg)\n\n![](images/a9/a923732241443a0fd6a5f53dab7de2d346f5704a0b034c276e64dc3df2746f4f.png)\n\n![](images/22/22a3429a802